From 0ee3b90b3bb599f9ce27799b3cc2e7b7920c7caa Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Luc3as <luc3as@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 6 Sep 2026 20:29:17 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 22/25] Harden the dashboard before the code goes public
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

An audit before publishing (bandit, ruff's S rules, pip-audit, detect-secrets,
and a read of the web layer against the OWASP list) found no injection, no
secrets, no vulnerable dependencies — and four things a dashboard on a home
network gets away with but a public repository should not ship:

- No CSRF defence. Forms authenticated by a cookie could be posted from any
  site the owner happened to visit. The cookie is now SameSite=Strict (and
  Secure behind HTTPS), state-changing requests whose Origin or Referer name
  another host are refused, and CSP adds form-action 'self'.
- No security headers. Every response now carries a CSP, X-Frame-Options
  DENY, nosniff, no-referrer and a Permissions-Policy.
- Inline onsubmit confirmations interpolated a search's name into a
  JavaScript string. Autoescaping protects HTML, not JavaScript-in-attribute:
  a name with a quote in it would have become code. Confirmations moved to
  data attributes read by one handler.
- The login form accepted guesses at network speed. Five wrong tokens from
  one address now earn a 60 s cooldown with Retry-After.

Also: the Telegram bot token is redacted from transport error messages
before they can reach the outbox, the logs or the History page. SECURITY.md
states the threat model, what is in place and what deliberately is not.
